16. Use the inbuilt dataset 'titanic'. The dataset contains 891 rows and
contains information about the passengers who boarded the unfortunate
Titanic ship. Write a code to check how the price of the ticket (column
name: 'fare') for each passenger is distributed by plotting a histogram.


# program16_titanic_fare_distribution.py
# Titanic dataset: Detailed fare distribution analysis + histogram

import seaborn as sns
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import pandas as pd

# ---------------- Load the inbuilt Titanic dataset ----------------
titanic = sns.load_dataset("titanic")

print("------ Titanic Dataset Loaded ------\n")
print(titanic.head(), "\n")
print("Shape:", titanic.shape, "\n")
print(titanic.info(), "\n")

# ---------------- Check 'fare' column data ----------------
print("\n------ Fare Column Analysis ------")
print("Number of missing values in 'fare':", titanic['fare'].isnull().sum())
print("\nSummary Statistics of Fare:")
print(titanic['fare'].describe(), "\n")

# ---------------- Histogram of Fare ----------------
plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))
plt.hist(titanic['fare'].dropna(), bins=30, edgecolor='black')
plt.xlabel("Fare Amount")
plt.ylabel("Frequency")
plt.title("Histogram: Distribution of Titanic Passenger Fare")
plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()

# ---------------- Histogram with KDE using seaborn (optional) ----------------
plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))
sns.histplot(titanic['fare'].dropna(), bins=30, kde=True)
plt.xlabel("Fare Amount")
plt.ylabel("Frequency")
plt.title("Fare Distribution with KDE (Smooth Curve)")
plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()

# ---------------- Interpretation ----------------
print("\n------ Interpretation of Fare Distribution ------")
print("1. Majority of passengers paid low fares (left-skewed distribution).")
print("2. Some passengers paid extremely high fares, creating a long right tail.")
print("3. KDE curve also shows that fare values are concentrated at lower ranges.")
print("4. High-fare outliers typically belonged to 1st class passengers.\n")
